 product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-kt){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\pi k-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi k & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-44093/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-44093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5d23d3cf-f562-55b6-a1a1-1b4a4146c492" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b7f0080d-47c8-51b3-bd21-ac49faf4fffe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0d0841b6-2d1d-5dab-a19e-108eb773ab25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "9d2a84b8-6764-5574-beb4-96645fee1169" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ebd49d25-d5cb-5d5d-80a1-41b98bc33285" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "85cda8ba-6b7d-5413-8098-1e67d942ffc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97452/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-97452/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-97452/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-97452/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-97452/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-97452/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97452" :question/id #uuid "b51342e3-c370-5f22-a8a0-d957bb6d3391" :question/math-academy-id "q-97452" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97452/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97452"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97451"] :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "eeb1a7da-962d-5399-b600-a33603a0219e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c838fff2-b351-501c-ab8c-8e747a5ab545" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6b0206e4-cb92-5d1b-b5f4-dd7c4fd6df0f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "722f90dc-693d-5e9b-9255-26611732b9f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6203d3e4-bcc8-5cab-a551-d201f4f09991" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "18b12b2c-0967-524d-a541-72ba430022aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97448/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-97448/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-97448/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-97448/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-97448/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-97448/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97448" :question/id #uuid "dec8198d-dc17-54f1-8d34-81db052f9f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97448" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97448/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97448"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8581"] :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/b8ece1b03ba237390f26e8d47e40013abc221edcae144ca3a9dc233438d486a3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/febc66857330a11bcd7bb93952cf5b86373974f5bf87690fdcf526d4939f19ec.png) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
